From: Adriel Santos <adriel@adriel.eu>
Subject: [PATCH] ath11k: clear stale LMAC ring pointers on in-place recovery

Since 32be3ca4cf78b ("wifi: ath11k: HAL SRNG: don't deinitialize and
re-initialize again") ath11k_core_reconfigure_on_crash() calls
ath11k_hal_srng_clear(), which keeps the shared rdp/wrp pointer buffers
and does not zero them. Its comment says the hw_init path clears every
slot, but LMAC rings return from ath11k_hal_srng_setup() before
ath11k_hal_srng_hw_init(), so their slots keep the previous firmware
instance's head/tail pointers. Without offload the host fills the RXDMA
buffer ring and rewrites its head pointer before HTT ring setup. With
NSS offload that fill is skipped, so the new firmware can be handed a
zeroed ring behind an NSS-written head pointer. This caused the IPQ5018
NOC error after in-place recovery with offload on (A/B below), and is the
likely cause of the QCN6122 zombie radio seen in the same setup.
The same fix was posted upstream (Kyle Farnung, "wifi: ath11k: clear
shared SRNG pointer state on restart", Fixes: 32be3ca4cf78b).

Zero rdp/wrp in ath11k_hal_srng_clear() and the LMAC slots again when
the ring is set up. Note that this also changes offload=0 recovery: the
host now posts a full RXDMA refill ring instead of about half of it.

Hardware A/B with NSS offload (2026-09-13, through a temporary switch
and diagnostic prints since removed): with the clear off, the RXDMA buffer
ring's head pointer was 198 at HTT ring setup and the Q6 raised a NOC error
0.6 s after recovery; with it on, 14/14 hw-restarts and every root-PD
assert recovered.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/hal.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/hal.c
@@ -1114,6 +1114,9 @@
 			srng->u.src_ring.hp_addr = (void *)(hal->wrp.vaddr +
 						   lmac_idx);
 			srng->flags |= HAL_SRNG_FLAGS_LMAC_RING;
+			/* LMAC rings skip hw_init: start from an empty ring */
+			*srng->u.src_ring.hp_addr = 0;
+			*srng->u.src_ring.tp_addr = 0;
 		} else {
 			if (!ab->hw_params.supports_shadow_regs)
 				srng->u.src_ring.hp_addr =
@@ -1147,6 +1150,9 @@
 			srng->u.dst_ring.tp_addr = (void *)(hal->wrp.vaddr +
 						   lmac_idx);
 			srng->flags |= HAL_SRNG_FLAGS_LMAC_RING;
+			/* LMAC rings skip hw_init: start from an empty ring */
+			*srng->u.dst_ring.tp_addr = 0;
+			*srng->u.dst_ring.hp_addr = 0;
 		} else {
 			if (!ab->hw_params.supports_shadow_regs)
 				srng->u.dst_ring.tp_addr =
@@ -1439,10 +1445,24 @@
 
 void ath11k_hal_srng_clear(struct ath11k_base *ab)
 {
-	/* No need to memset rdp and wrp memory since each individual
-	 * segment would get cleared in ath11k_hal_srng_src_hw_init()
-	 * and ath11k_hal_srng_dst_hw_init().
+	struct ath11k_hal *hal = &ab->hal;
+
+	/*
+	 * Keep the shared pointer buffers allocated, but drop the previous
+	 * firmware instance's hp/tp values. Non-LMAC rings get their slots
+	 * zeroed in ath11k_hal_srng_{src,dst}_hw_init(), LMAC rings do not:
+	 * their host-owned pointer is only rewritten when the host itself
+	 * fills the ring. With NSS offload the RXDMA buffer ring is filled
+	 * by NSS after HTT ring setup, so the new firmware would otherwise
+	 * see the old head pointer over a zeroed ring.
 	 */
+	if (hal->rdp.vaddr)
+		memset(hal->rdp.vaddr, 0,
+		       sizeof(*hal->rdp.vaddr) * HAL_SRNG_RING_ID_MAX);
+	if (hal->wrp.vaddr)
+		memset(hal->wrp.vaddr, 0,
+		       sizeof(*hal->wrp.vaddr) * HAL_SRNG_NUM_LMAC_RINGS);
+
 	memset(ab->hal.srng_list, 0,
 	       sizeof(ab->hal.srng_list));
 	memset(ab->hal.shadow_reg_addr, 0,
